.\" Manpage for ttrack.
.TH TTRACK 1 "2026-05-26" "ttrack 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ttrack \- record and replay Linux terminal sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ttrack rec
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RI [ command
.RI [ args ...]]
.br
.B ttrack play
.RB [ \-\-speed
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-idle
.IR N ]
.I file
.br
.B ttrack ls
.br
.B ttrack help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ttrack
records an interactive terminal session and replays it later.
It launches a command (a shell by default) inside a pseudo-terminal
(PTY), forwards your keystrokes to it, and copies everything the
program prints to both your screen and a recording file. The recording
can then be replayed with original timing.
.PP
It is a single self-contained binary, comparable to
.BR script (1)
or
.BR asciinema (1),
and writes the widely supported
.B asciinema v2
"cast" format, so recordings also play with
.B asciinema play
when that tool is installed.
.PP
.B ttrack
records terminal
.I output
only; it does not store your keystrokes. It records only the session
you explicitly start with
.BR "ttrack rec" .
There is no daemon, no system-wide login hook, and no remote storage.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B rec \fR(alias: \fBrecord\fR)
Record a session. Runs
.I command
under a PTY; with no
.IR command ,
runs the user's login shell (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
Recording stops when the command exits (type
.B exit
or press Ctrl-D in a shell). The output file path is printed on exit.
.TP
.B play
Replay a recorded
.I file
to the current terminal, reproducing the original inter-event timing.
.TP
.B ls \fR(alias: \fBlist\fR)
List recordings in the store directory, showing file name, start time,
and the recorded command.
.TP
.B help
Print a short usage summary. Same as
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS rec options
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the recording to
.IR file .
By default a file is auto-named in the store directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.SS play options
.TP
.BI \-\-speed " N"
Playback speed multiplier. Default
.BR 1.0 .
Values above 1 play faster (e.g.
.B 2.0
is twice as fast); values below 1 play slower. Must be greater than 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-idle " N"
Cap idle gaps between events to at most
.I N
seconds, so long pauses in the original session do not stall playback.
Default
.BR 2.0 .
Set to
.B 0
to disable the cap and replay with exact original timing.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TTRACK_DIR
Directory where recordings are stored and listed. If unset, defaults to
.IR ~/.local/share/ttrack .
.TP
.B SHELL
Shell launched by
.B ttrack rec
when no
.I command
is given. If unset,
.I /bin/bash
is used.
.TP
.B TERM
Recorded in the cast header so players can reproduce terminal behavior.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/ttrack/
Default store directory (overridden by
.BR TTRACK_DIR ).
.TP
.I <store>/<YYYYMMDDThhmmss>-<pid>.cast
Auto-named recording. The timestamp is the session start time; the pid
is the recorder's process id.
.SH FILE FORMAT
Recordings use the asciinema v2 cast format: a UTF-8, JSON-lines text
file.
.PP
The first line is a header object, for example:
.PP
.RS
.EX
{"version":2,"width":80,"height":24,"timestamp":1779776263,
 "command":"/bin/bash","env":{"SHELL":"/bin/bash","TERM":"xterm-256color"}}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Each subsequent line is an event array
.RB [ "time, \"o\", data" ]
where
.I time
is seconds (float) since session start,
.B "o"
marks terminal output, and
.I data
is the printed text:
.PP
.RS
.EX
[0.131000, "o", "hello\\r\\n"]
.EE
.RE
.PP
Because the format is standard asciinema v2, files are also viewable
with
.B asciinema cat
and playable with
.BR "asciinema play" .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Record an interactive shell, then replay it:
.EX
.B $ ttrack rec
.B $ ttrack ls
.B $ ttrack play ~/.local/share/ttrack/20260526T120422-1858012.cast
.EE
.TP
Record a single command:
.EX
.B $ ttrack rec /bin/bash -c 'make test'
.EE
.TP
Record to a named file:
.EX
.B $ ttrack rec -o /tmp/deploy.cast
.EE
.TP
Replay at double speed:
.EX
.B $ ttrack play --speed 2 session.cast
.EE
.TP
Replay with exact original pauses (no idle cap):
.EX
.B $ ttrack play --idle 0 session.cast
.EE
.TP
Store recordings in a custom directory:
.EX
.B $ TTRACK_DIR=/var/log/sessions ttrack rec
.B $ TTRACK_DIR=/var/log/sessions ttrack ls
.EE
.TP
Play with the standard asciinema player, if installed:
.EX
.B $ asciinema play session.cast
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. Note:
.B ttrack rec
exits 0 once the session is recorded, regardless of the exit status of
the recorded command.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error (e.g.
.B play
given a missing or malformed file, or the recorder could not start the
command).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (unknown subcommand or bad arguments).
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
Records terminal output only; keystrokes (input) are not captured.
.IP \(bu 2
Records only the session started by
.BR "ttrack rec" ;
it does not attach to existing sessions or auto-record logins.
.IP \(bu 2
No daemon, PAM integration, access control, or remote storage. Recording
files are written with the invoking user's permissions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR script (1),
.BR scriptreplay (1),
.BR asciinema (1),
.BR ttyrec (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Part of the terminal-session-recorder project.
